     "<div dir=\"ltr\">To add to Alex&#39;s answer, the default behaviour of the sem() function is to include residual covariances for endogenous variables that do not also appear on the right-hand side of a &quot;~&quot; in the model specification.<div><br></div><div>For example, if you had</div><div><br></div><div><div>mod2 &lt;- &#39;</div><div>A ~ B</div><div>C ~ B</div><div>D ~ A + C</div><div>&#39;</div><div><br></div><div>then sem() would not automatically include A ~~ C.</div><div><br></div><div>The model you were trying to specify is</div><div><br></div><div></div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><div><div><font color=\"#000000\">mod1 &lt;-&#39;</font></div><div><font color=\"#000000\">A ~ a1*B</font></div><div><font color=\"#000000\">C ~ a2*B</font></div></div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><div><div><font color=\"#000000\">A ~~ 0*C</font></div><div><font color=\"#000000\">&#39;</font></div></div></div><div><font color=\"#000000\"><br></font></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "dIG0hgcdBQAJ",
   "from": "Christopher Bratt",
   "author": "572119bb37de95d7",
   "date": "2018-03-23T16:46:43Z",
   "subject": "Re: lavaan removing degree of freedom? (unspecified endogenous covariance)",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "It is possible to fix the residual covariance to zero in this model, but you probably shouldn&#39;t. <p>Is really all the covariance between the two dependent variables explained by the predictor? I guess not. But the models will be nested and can be tested as such. </p>"
    ]
   ]
  }
 ]
}
